A point $(x,y)$ is closer to $O=(0,0)$ than to $(4,4)$ when
\[
x^2+y^2<(x-4)^2+(y-4)^2\iff 0<-8x-8y+32\iff x+y<4 ,
\]
i.e.\ it lies on the origin's side of the perpendicular bisector $x+y=4$ of the segment from $O$ to $(4,4)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0),P=(4,4);
fill((0,1)--(3,1)--(0,4)--cycle,lightgray);
draw((-0.6,0)--(6,0),Arrow(TeXHead));draw((0,-0.6)--(0,6.8),Arrow(TeXHead));
draw((0,1)--(5,1)--(5,6)--(0,6)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw((0,4)--(4.6,-0.6),dashed+blue);
draw(O--P,dotted);
dot(O);dot(P);dot((3,1));dot((0,4));
label("$O$",O,SW);label("$(4,4)$",P,N);
label("$(3,1)$",(3,1),dir(-125));label("$(0,4)$",(0,4),W);label("$(0,1)$",(0,1),W);label("$(5,6)$",(5,6),NE);
label("$x+y=4$",(4.6,-0.6),SE,blue);
label("$x$",(6,0),N);label("$y$",(0,6.8),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The region is the $5\times5$ square $0\le x\le5$, $1\le y\le6$ of area $25$. The part of it with $x+y<4$ is the shaded right triangle with vertices $(0,1)$, $(3,1)$, $(0,4)$, with legs $3$ and $3$ and area $\dfrac{9}{2}$. The probability is
\[
\dfrac{\ \dfrac{9}{2}\ }{25}=\dfrac{9}{50}=\boxed{18\%}.
\]

Count by the units digit.
\begin{itemize}
\item Units digit $0$: the hundreds digit has $9$ choices and the tens digit $8$: $72$.
\item Units digit $2$, $4$, $6$ or $8$: the hundreds digit cannot be $0$ or the units digit ($8$ choices) and the tens digit has $8$ choices: $4\cdot64=256$.
\end{itemize}
Total $72+256=\boxed{328}$.

question:
How many $3$-digit even numbers can be written using the digits $0,1,2,3,4,5,6$,
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] if digits may be repeated;
\item[(ii)] if all three digits must be different?
\end{enumerate}

The $5$ diagonals of a convex pentagon are drawn. They meet in $10$ points: the $5$ vertices (where two diagonals meet) and the $5$ points where two diagonals cross inside the pentagon.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
